          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#recalling_the_context recalling the context
          %p
            The undertaking called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            started out as
            %em perturbative string theory
            where the idea was to encode
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            that is obtained by a sum of the integrals of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            of a fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+superconformal+field+theory"} 2d superconformal field theory
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
            of conformal structures on surfaces of all possible genera – thought of as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            elements obtained this way from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
            could be seen to be approximated by an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective QFT
            (some flavor of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            and
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
          %p
            (The
            %em first superstring revolution
            was given by the realization that this makes sense: the effective background theories obtained this way are indeed free of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomalies
          %p
            Hence it is the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            which in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            translates products of “field insertions” into
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            it is the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
            which does this, and therefore
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
            are the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory+vacua"} perturbative string theory vacua
          %h3#narrowing_in_on_the_issue narrowing in on the issue
          %p
            The
            %em second superstring revolution
            was given by the realization that all these background field theories seem to fit into one single bigger context that seems to exists independently of their perturbative definitions.
          %p
            Aspects of this bigger non-perturbative context are known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
          %p
            In a different but similar manner, other background field theories were found whose classical solutions are thought to encode “stable solutions” (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            solutions”) of whatever physical theory this non-perturbative definition of string theory is.
          %p
            Here, when talking about a “stable solution” one thinks of solutions of these theories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            with plenty of extra fields that look like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            times something else, such that all these extra fields are constant in time (using the simple Minkowsi-space-times-internal-part-ansatz to say what “constant in time” means), hence sitting at the bottom of their corresponding effective potentials.
          %p
            Solutions with this property, in particular for all the scalar fields that appear, are said to have
            %em stabilized moduli
            \: the scalar fields that encode various properties of the geometry of the solution are constant in time.
          %p
            Since these geometric properties determine, in the fashion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+theory"} Kaluza-Klein theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            factor, it is these “moduli-stabilized” solutions that have a first chance of being candidate solutions of whatever that theory is we are talking about, which describe the real world.
          %h3#the_landscape the landscape
          %p
            At some point there had been the hope that only very few such solutions exist. When arguments were put forward that this is far from being true, the term
            %strong landscape
            for the collection of all such solutions was invented.
          %p So, to summarize in a few words, the landscape of string theory vacua is…
          %h2#flux_compactifications Flux compactifications
          %p
            One widely studied class of moduli-stabilized solutions to the string-theory background equations is that of
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+compactifications"} flux compactifications
          %p
            These are classical solutions to the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theory that are of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %mi CY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4 \times CY
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CY
            some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            of six real dimensions such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            in the solution has nontrivial values on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CY
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CY
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fluxes"} fluxes
            \.
          %p
            The presence of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            in the solution induces an effective potential for the scalar moduli fields that parameterize the geometry of CY. Hence by choosing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            suitably one can find classical solutions in which all these moduli have values that are constant in time.
          %p
            A review of flux compactifications is for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Grana05"}> Graña 05
            )
          %h2#computer_scans_of_gepner_model_compactifications Computer scans of Gepner model compactifications
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactified
            with non-geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
            such that the would-be string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
            with these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spaces"} target spaces
            are in fact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gepner+models"} Gepner models
            (in the sense of
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://www.physicsforums.com/insights/spectral-standard-model-string-compactifications/"} Spectral Standard Model and String Compactifications
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DijkstraHuiszoonSchellekens04a"} Dijkstra-Huiszoon-Schellekens 04a
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#DijkstraHuiszoonSchellekens04b"} Dijkstra-Huiszoon-Schellekens 04b
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/SchellekensSuperGepnerModelScanII.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              A plot of
              = succeed "-like" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
              in a computer scan of
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gepner+model"} Gepner model
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
              according to
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#DijkstraHuiszoonSchellekens04b"} Dijkstra-Huiszoon-Schellekens 04b
            %p
              The blue dot indicates the couplings in
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SU
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 5
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(5)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} GUT
              theory. The faint lines are NOT drawn by hand, but reflect increased density of Gepner models as seen by the computer scan.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
